{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.BoundedOrderWronskian","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.BoundedOrderWronskian","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.MvGenWronskian","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPolynomial.MonomialOrder.DegLex","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Polynomial.UniqueFactorization"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.linearIndependent_iff_exists_bounded_order_mvGenWronskian_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.linearIndependent_iff_exists_bounded_order_mvGenWronskian_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":380,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L380-L397","doc":"**Independence is exactly a non-vanishing bounded-order generalized Wronskian.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_bounded_order_mvGenWronskian_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_bounded_order_mvGenWronskian_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":364,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L364-L378","doc":"**An independent family has a non-zero generalized Wronskian of BOUNDED ORDER.**\n\n`ord (Δᵢ) ≤ i` — the form Roth's lemma consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_dependence_of_forall_bounded_mvGenWronskian_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_dependence_of_forall_bounded_mvGenWronskian_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":264,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L264-L362","doc":"**Route B: the bounded-order criterion for general `m`.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_constant_dependence_of_bounded_wronskians_vanish","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_constant_dependence_of_bounded_wronskians_vanish","kind":"theorem","line":229,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L229-L261","doc":"**Collapse to constants.**  Once every Wronskian `c_l ∂ₖc_j − c_j ∂ₖc_l` vanishes and\n`c_l ≠ 0`, the polynomial coefficient vector is a constant multiple of `c_l`, and the\nconstant vector is non-zero because its `l`-th entry is `1`.\n\nMirrors `Polynomial.exists_constant_dependence_of_wronskians_vanish`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_wronskian_combo_bounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_wronskian_combo_bounded","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L210-L227","doc":"**The Wronskian combination, bounded-order version.**\n\n`∑ⱼ (c_l ∂ₖc_j − c_j ∂ₖc_l) Δ_l fⱼ = 0`, and its `l`-th entry vanishes identically.  Mirrors\n`Polynomial.sum_wronskian_combo_eq_zero`: multiplying by `c_l` where the classical proof\ndivides is what keeps everything inside the polynomial ring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_pderiv_descent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_pderiv_descent","kind":"theorem","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L184-L207","doc":"**Derivative descent for bounded-order operators.**\n\nIf `c` annihilates every operator of order `≤ n`, then `∂ₖc` annihilates every operator of\norder `< n`.  Mirrors `Polynomial.sum_derivative_of_forall_sum_eq_zero`; the reason it works\nis that `∂ₖ ∘ Δ_l = Δ_{k::l}` has order `|l| + 1 ≤ n`, so the cross term is already zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_dependence_of_forall_wronskian_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_dependence_of_forall_wronskian_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L163-L181","doc":"Restatement of the `m = 2` case in the shape the induction consumes: with `f ≠ 0`, the\ncoefficient of `g` can be taken non-zero, so `g` is a constant multiple of `f`.\n\nMirrors `Polynomial.exists_dependence_of_wronskian_eq_zero`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_bounded_order_mvGenWronskian_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_bounded_order_mvGenWronskian_two","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L117-L138","doc":"**The `m = 2` bounded-order criterion, in the committed shape.**\n\nThe family `(id, ∂ₖ)` has orders `0` and `1`, which is exactly `ord (Δᵢ) ≤ i`. So for two\npolynomials the bounded-order generalized Wronskian criterion holds outright. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_const_dependence_of_forall_wronskian_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_const_dependence_of_forall_wronskian_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L71-L115","doc":"**If every first-order directional Wronskian vanishes, the pair is proportional over the\nconstants.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.totalDegree_pderiv_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.totalDegree_pderiv_lt","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L51-L68","doc":"**Differentiating strictly drops the total degree.**\n\nMathlib has the univariate `Polynomial.natDegree_derivative_lt`; this is the multivariate\nstatement.  Companion to `degreeOf_pderiv_lt` in `QOU.Mathlib.MvGenWronskian`; the total\ndegree is the one that pairs with `totalDegree_mul_of_isDomain`, which is why both exist. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_add_single_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_add_single_one","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BoundedOrderWronskian.lean#L45-L49","signatureChunk":0}]}